// engine/level/bg.emp — Plane B (background) drawing routines (§2 A.5)
//
// T1 (zone-wide):     BG_Init loads act_bg_tiles into the shared BG VRAM region
//                     (slots 1024-1471, $8000-$B7FF — bounded above by the
//                     relocated SAT at $B800; see BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES below),
//                     then blits act_bg_layout into Plane B nametable. Both
//                     happen once at level load.
// PER-REGION:         Section_RedrawPlanes (in section.emp) blits the layout of
//                     the REGION under the camera centre at level init and cache
//                     recovery only, with Act.act_bg_layout as the fallback when
//                     the region's rg_bg_layout is 0. Teleports no longer redraw.
//
// THE BACKGROUND BELONGS TO THE REGION, NOT TO THE SECTION (regions part 2,
// step 3, 2026-09-16; spec §4.5). `Sec.sec_bg_layout` is DELETED — Sec 26 -> 22
// bytes — and Section_RedrawPlanes now resolves through Region_Resolve on the
// camera centre, the same point and the same query Parallax_CheckBoundary's
// crossing and the init ladder's parallax boot select already use. Three private
// answers to "which background" would have been three chances to disagree; the
// 2026-08-26 precedence bug is what that looks like when it happens.
//
// Layout shape: each nametable layout is a raw 64x64 = 8192 bytes (full
// Plane B nametable — the BG is 512px tall for vertical parallax; the
// injector zero-pads 32-row layouts). Byte order is ROW-MAJOR:
// blob[(row*64 + col)*2] — each row's 64 columns are contiguous (row stride
// = 64 cols * 2 B = 128), which is BYTE-FOR-BYTE THE PLANE B NAMETABLE'S OWN
// VRAM ORDER. So a full-plane blit is one linear move.l run at autoinc $02
// (BG_Init below; Section_RedrawPlanes' Plane B half is the same run), and one
// plane ROW is a contiguous 64-word gather (Draw_BG_TileRow, engine/level/
// plane_buffer.emp). Both out-of-assembler writers now emit this order
// directly — tools/ojz_strip_gen.py always did, and tools/inject_editor_bg.py
// (which overwrites strip_gen's intermediate) stopped transposing.
//
// IT WAS COLUMN-MAJOR UNTIL REGIONS PART 2, STEP 2 (2026-09-15), and the flip
// is option R of that spec's §4.2. Column contiguity existed for one consumer,
// `Draw_BG_TileColumn`, which had ZERO callers for its whole life and is
// deleted in the same step; the column path returns in the big-levels parcel
// over a block map, not over this raw blob. What part 2 needs is the other
// axis: a background taller than the plane streams in by ROW, and the repaint
// at a region crossing sweeps row by row. Against a column-major blob a row
// producer is a 64-read strided gather at stride 128 — about twice the cost,
// on every streamed row, forever — while the two live blits had to be rewritten
// under part 2 regardless (after the plane shrink neither can drain a whole
// 8192-byte layout into a 4096-byte plane; each blits a WINDOW of
// PLANE_V_CELLS rows, which is a row loop by nature).
//
// ALL 64 ROWS ARE LIVE (NEW-5 close, 2026-08-05). Both runtime maintainers of
// Plane B cover the full plane — BG_Init and Section_RedrawPlanes each drain
// the whole BG_LAYOUT_SIZE image. The old 32-row maintainer limit silently
// half-reverted any BG art above row 31 on a redraw; that was NOT
// hypothetical: the shipped OJZ override carries real art in rows 32-63, and
// the full-plane vscroll wrap (v_factor_bg with a 512px plane) puts those
// rows on screen, including at spawn. The 512px vertical headroom the shape
// advertises is therefore real, in every writer.
// Tile-blob shape: 2-byte big-endian length header + raw deduped tile bytes
//   (mirrors S4LZ blob shape so engine can read length without a separate field).
// Decision rationale: docs/research/per-section-background.md (Q4 + Q5).

module engine.bg in bg

use engine.constants.{VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE, VRAM_PLANE_B_BYTES, BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, BG_TILE_CAPACITY, VDP_DATA, VDP_CTRL, PLANE_H_CELLS, PLANE_V_CELLS}
use engine.constants.{SCREEN_HEIGHT}
use engine.structs.{Act, Region}
use engine.vdp.{VdpTarget, VdpOp, vdp_comm}
use engine.z80_bus.{z80_stopped}
// Regions part 2 step 5. The streamer reads the clamped map-space BG scroll that
// Parallax_Step5_Vscroll produced THIS frame, and its per-frame row budget is
// derived from that step's rate clamp rather than chosen. engine.parallax does
// not import this module, so the edge is acyclic.
use engine.parallax.{BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX, BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP}
use engine.plane_buffer.{Draw_BG_TileRow}

// `pub` because it is a CONTRACT, not an internal: the act's BG blob must be
// exactly this long, and games/sonic4/data/levels/ojz/act1/act_assets.emp now
// types its embed with it so a wrong-sized blob is an `array length mismatch` at
// build time rather than a transposed background at runtime (tools lens sweep D6).
pub const BG_LAYOUT_SIZE  = 64*64*2     // 8192 bytes (full Plane B nametable)
// The shared BG tile region runs from BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM up to the relocated
// sprite attr table at $B800 — so $8000..$B7FF = 448 tiles is the PHYSICAL run.
// It is no longer the clamp: the top of that run has been carved away by other
// regions, so the BG arena owns only BG_TILE_CAPACITY of it and a full 448-tile
// blob would spray over its neighbours' art rather than over the SAT. The clamp
// below uses the declared capacity; this constant stays because the `ensure`
// under it is what keeps the two facts from crossing.
//
// NO FIGURE IS STATED HERE ON PURPOSE. This comment twice said "400" and twice
// named a slot range, and both went stale under carves it never saw: the arena
// went 448 -> 400 (waterline strips) -> 388 -> 376 (the spring, in two edits),
// while the prose sat still. A fresh literal would stale on the identical clock.
// The live values are `BG_TILE_CAPACITY` itself (asserted against vram.toml by
// the game's own `ensure`) and the generated map, `docs/generated/vram-map-<game>.md`
// — read one of those, never a sentence.
const BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES = VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE - BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM   // $3800 = 14336 B = 448 tiles
const BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES = BG_TILE_CAPACITY * 32                 // the arena's declared size in bytes; see above for why no total is written here
ensure(BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES <= BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES,
       "games/<game>/vram.toml declares bg_region larger than the physical $8000..$B7FF run: BG_TILE_CAPACITY = {BG_TILE_CAPACITY} tiles = {BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES} bytes against {BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES}. The BG arena is bounded above by the relocated sprite attribute table at VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE, so a capacity past it is not a bigger arena, it is a blob clamp that lets act art overwrite the SAT")

// -----------------------------------------------
// BG_Init — load shared BG tiles + blit Plane B nametable.
//
// In:  a0 = Act descriptor pointer (read only — preserved)
// Out: none
// Clobbers: d0, d1, a1, a2
//
// Display assumed off (called from Level_LoadArt, before display enable).
// Both writes are blocking via VDP DATA port; autoincrement = 2 bytes/word.
// The Z80 bus is held for the full copy brackets (~21ms + ~12ms worst case)
// — music already playing through level load takes an audible DAC gap
// (accepted at load; the VBlank flag-bracket idiom is the alternative if
// load-time audio continuity ever matters).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BG_Init (a0: *Act) clobbers(d0-d1/a1-a2) preserves(a3/sr) {
        movem.l a3, -(sp)
        movea.l a0, a3                  // a3 = act ptr (preserve)
        // Mask interrupts for the blocking VDP copies: a VBlank mid-copy lets
        // the VInt handler repoint the VDP address and the remainder of the
        // blob sprays to wrong addresses (large BG blobs span >1 frame of
        // copy loop). Mirrors Section_RedrawPlanes' guard.
        move.w  sr, -(sp)
        move.w  #$2700, sr

        // --- load BG tile blob into shared region at BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM ---
        movea.l Act.act_bg_tiles(a3), a1
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        beq     .skip_tiles
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // d1.w = tile-bytes length (uncompressed)
        beq     .skip_tiles
        // Capacity guard (last line of defense): clamp the copy length to what the
        // BG arena is DECLARED to own — BG_TILE_CAPACITY tiles, not the physical
        // $8000..$B7FF run. Above the arena sit `waterline_strips` (48 tiles, item
        // 9d) and then the SAT at $B800, and a blob longer than the declared
        // capacity would overwrite the first before it ever reached the second.
        // The build tools should keep BG art within budget (inject_editor_bg.py
        // gates the final blob on the same constant); this is what guarantees no
        // corruption of either neighbour if a generator gate drifts.
        cmpi.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d1
        bls     .bg_len_ok
        move.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d1
    .bg_len_ok:
        // Tier-1 blit: copy the tile body a LONGWORD at a time (autoinc 2 keeps
        // it linear in VRAM). The count is quartered, so the length guard must be
        // RE-DERIVED — the parcel-6 word-count guard (lsr #1) does not survive the
        // move.l translation, it just underflows on a different set of lengths.
        //
        // The clamp above bounds the copy from ABOVE. It does NOT bound it from
        // below: a drifted length of 1..3 bytes passes the `beq` (nonzero) and the
        // `bls` (in range), then lsr.w #2 -> 0 and subq -> $FFFF, and dbf would run
        // 65,536 LONGWORDS = 256KB straight through the SAT and every other VRAM
        // structure. Close it by taking the LONGWORD count FIRST and skipping on
        // zero: lengths 1,2,3 all lsr#2 -> 0 -> `beq` skips BEFORE the subq, so the
        // underflow is unreachable. Lengths >= 4 copy floor(len/4) longwords and
        // drop the sub-longword tail (len 5 -> 1 longword + 1 dropped byte). Real
        // blobs are 32-byte granular, and BOTH producers now assert len%4==0 —
        // ojz_strip_gen.py (the unconditional emitter; assert added NEW-4
        // 2026-08-05 — the old comment here credited only the injector, which
        // runs solely when an editor override exists) and inject_editor_bg.py
        // — so the drop never fires in practice; it is the last line of defense.
        //
        // Both counter ops sit ABOVE the z80_stopped bracket on purpose:
        // .skip_tiles is past the bracket's release, so a guard branch taken from
        // inside it would leave the Z80 halted for the rest of the level — the
        // bracket refuses that shape outright (`[context.escape]`). Neither the
        // bus request nor the VDP setup touches d1, so the hoist is free. (No
        // assert on the drop: the debug expansion would push
        // .skip_tiles past short-branch reach and force shape-dependent width
        // pins — ledgered instead; the emitter always emits 32-byte multiples.)
        lsr.w   #2, d1                  // longword count = bytes/4
        beq     .skip_tiles             // 1..3-byte (sub-long) blob -> nothing to copy
        subq.w  #1, d1                  // dbf counter (safe: longword count >= 1)
        with z80_stopped {
            move.w  #$8F02, VDP_CTRL        // (VDP_CTRL).l — autoincrement = 2
            move.l  #vdp_comm(BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, VdpTarget.Vram, VdpOp.Write), VDP_CTRL
            lea     VDP_DATA, a2            // (VDP_DATA).l
        .tile_copy:
            move.l  (a1)+, (a2)
            dbf     d1, .tile_copy
        }
    .skip_tiles:

        // --- blit BG nametable to Plane B ---
        movea.l Act.act_bg_layout(a3), a1
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        beq     .skip_nt

        // THE TRACKER SEED, AND WHY IT NAMES THE ACT DEFAULT AND NOT A REGION.
        // The step-3 spec (§4.5, boot half) asks this routine to resolve the boot
        // region first and blit THAT row's layout, on the stated premise that
        // "Camera_Init precedes the boot select". That premise is true of the
        // PARALLAX boot select and false of this routine, MEASURED at the one
        // shipped ladder (games/sonic4/test/ojz_scroll_test.emp): `jbsr
        // Level_LoadArt` — which tail-calls this proc from engine/level/load_art.emp
        // — stands ~48 lines ABOVE `jbsr Camera_Init`, and Camera_Init is the only
        // writer of Camera_X/Y before the plane fill. A Region_Resolve here would
        // therefore query the camera centre of a camera that does not exist yet:
        // (0,0) + half a screen after boot's RAM clear, or the PREVIOUS act's
        // position on a re-entry. It would answer, it would answer wrongly, and
        // today it would answer wrongly in a way no picture could show, because
        // all ten of act 1's rows default their layout. That is the shape this
        // parcel's step-2 sibling refused: an instrument that cannot produce the
        // answer it claims to look for.
        //
        // Nothing is lost by declining. The display is still OFF here (this runs
        // from Level_LoadArt, before display enable), and the region-aware blit —
        // Section_RedrawPlanes, reached through Section_Plane_Dirty — runs later in
        // the SAME init, after Camera_Init and after the DEBUG boot-position
        // override, and re-seeds all four cells. So the first VISIBLE frame is the
        // region's picture on every path, and the invariant the later steps rest on
        // ("the tracker names the blob the plane holds") is true at every instant in
        // between rather than from the second writer onwards.
        //
        // Booked in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md as BG-BOOT-REGION-BLIT, which is also
        // where the ordering fix belongs if a future act ever needs the FIRST blit
        // to be the right one.
        move.l  a1, BG_Plane_Layout             // (BG_Plane_Layout).w — what the plane will hold
        clr.w   BG_Plane_Top                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w — window top = map row 0
        clr.w   BG_Wipe_Cursor                  // (BG_Wipe_Cursor).w — cursor + dir, one word
        // Second consumer of the $2700 mask above. The mask is STILL load-bearing
        // after the row-major flip, and for the reason that outlived the
        // autoincrement excursion: the VInt handler repoints the VDP address
        // register, so a VBlank landing inside this 8192-byte blocking copy sends
        // the remainder of the image to whatever address the handler left behind.
        // (The excursion itself is gone — the blit now runs at the default autoinc
        // $02 from first word to last, so Flush_VDP_Shadow's every-VBlank reg $0F
        // re-blit can no longer contradict it. That removes one of the two hazards
        // the mask covers, not both.)
        if DEBUG == 1 {
            move.w  sr, d0
            andi.w  #$0700, d0
            assert.w d0, hs, #$0600     // IPL >= 6: no VBlank can land mid-copy
        }
        with z80_stopped {
            // ROW-MAJOR blit, and it is a straight linear copy: the layout blob's
            // byte order (blob[(row*64 + col)*2]) IS the Plane B nametable's VRAM
            // order, so the whole BG_LAYOUT_SIZE image drains as one autoinc-$02
            // move.l run with a single VDP command setup. The 64 per-column command
            // setups the column-major blit needed are gone with it.
            //
            // No length guard needed: the geometry is the compile-time constant
            // BG_LAYOUT_SIZE, never derived from blob data, so the sub-longword
            // underflow that bites the tile blit above cannot occur — the layout
            // blob is a fixed 8192 B image by contract (act_assets.emp types the
            // embed with BG_LAYOUT_SIZE, so a wrong-sized blob is a build error).
            //
            // The count is BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1 = 2047, which is past a moveq's
            // signed byte, so it loads with a move.w. Do not "optimise" it back.
            move.w  #$8F02, VDP_CTRL       // (VDP_CTRL).l — autoincrement 2 (one cell)
            move.l  #vdp_comm(VRAM_PLANE_B_BYTES, VdpTarget.Vram, VdpOp.Write), VDP_CTRL
            lea     VDP_DATA, a2           // (VDP_DATA).l
            move.w  #BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1, d0
        .nt_copy:
            move.l  (a1)+, (a2)
            dbf     d0, .nt_copy
        }
    .skip_nt:
        move.w  (sp)+, sr               // restore interrupt mask
        movem.l (sp)+, a3
        rts
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE STEADY-STATE ROW STREAMER (regions part 2, step 5; spec §4.2, "Steady-state
// streaming"). Plane B stops being the whole background picture and becomes a
// 64-row WINDOW onto a taller map, exactly the way Plane A is already "a 64-cell
// ring the world wraps through" (engine/level/section.emp).
//
// THE RING IS FREE, AND THAT IS THE ONE FACT TO READ FIRST. The VDP maps screen
// line L to plane line (L + vscroll) mod PLANE_B_SPAN, so the plane row showing
// map row m is ALWAYS m mod PLANE_V_CELLS — no extra arithmetic, no rebasing, no
// mapping table. The whole of streaming is therefore one question: which 64
// consecutive MAP rows does the plane currently hold? That number is
// `BG_Plane_Top` and this proc is its only mover after the two synchronous blits
// seed it.
//
// ---- THE WINDOW RULE, AND ITS CONTAINMENT PROOF ----
//
//   want_top = clamp( (vscroll >> 3) - BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS,  0,  map_rows - PLANE_V_CELLS )
//
// It is ANCHORED ON THE SCROLL, not hysteretic ("move only when a visible row
// falls off"). Both keep the picture correct; the anchored form was chosen for
// three reasons and the third is the one that matters:
//   1. No direction state and no edge cases — one formula, both ways.
//   2. The lead is centred, so there is slack in BOTH directions. A hysteretic
//      tracker has ZERO slack in the direction of travel by construction: the row
//      that must be drawn is the row that just became visible, so a single
//      refused entry (plane buffer full that frame) is a garbage row ON SCREEN.
//      With a centred lead a refusal is invisible and self-heals next frame.
//   3. It absorbs a region crossing. Cross into a row whose map is taller and
//      `max_top` jumps 0 -> 32 in one frame, so `want_top` can jump by up to the
//      lead at once. The containment proof below holds at every intermediate
//      `BG_Plane_Top` on the way, so the catch-up is invisible rather than being
//      a tear that step 6's wipe has to hide.
//
// CONTAINMENT, derived (this is what the three `ensure`s below pin). The screen
// shows map rows [vrow, vbot] where vrow = vscroll >> 3 and vbot <= vrow +
// BG_SCREEN_ROWS - 1. The plane holds [top, top + PLANE_V_CELLS - 1]. Need
// top <= vrow and top + PLANE_V_CELLS - 1 >= vbot, i.e.
//     0 <= BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS <= PLANE_V_CELLS - BG_SCREEN_ROWS.
// At the low clamp (top = 0): vrow <= LEAD, so vbot <= LEAD + ROWS-1 <= 63. At the
// high clamp (top = map_rows - PLANE_V_CELLS): the largest legal vscroll is
// span - SCREEN_HEIGHT (Parallax_Step5_Vscroll's position clamp), whose vbot is
// exactly map_rows - 1 = top + PLANE_V_CELLS - 1. The two clamps meet the two
// bounds EXACTLY, which is why the position clamp of step 4 had to land first.
//
// ---- WHY IT CANNOT FALL BEHIND, WHICH IS STEP 4's RATE CLAMP DOING THIS JOB ----
// `want_top` is a monotone function of `vscroll` with slope 1/8, and step 4 bounds
// |dvscroll| by BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP per frame. So |dwant_top| <= BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP
// / BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX = BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS rows per frame, which is exactly this
// proc's per-frame budget. The tracker therefore NEVER accumulates debt: it is not
// "fast enough in practice", it is bounded by construction, and the `ensure` below
// is what keeps the two constants tied together if either moves.
//
// ---- WHAT THIS PROC DOES NOT DO, named so a green is not misread ----
// It is the STEADY-STATE half only. A PRIME — the plane holding the wrong window
// outright — is not reachable from here at 2 rows a frame, and there is one path
// that can produce one: `Section_RedrawPlanes` (cache recovery, and the DEBUG warp)
// re-blits map rows 0..63 and re-seeds `BG_Plane_Top` to 0 whatever the scroll is.
// On every map that is exactly the plane's height that is a no-op; on a TALLER map
// with the scroll away from the top it leaves a wrong window that this proc walks
// back at BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS rows a frame. That is the BG-PLANE-WINDOW item booked
// in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md (step 3 opened it, step 8 makes it mandatory) and it is
// NOT closed here: closing it means a windowed two-run blit at both writers, and
// the number it needs does not exist at `BG_Init` (Parallax_Init runs after both
// blits and zeroes the scroll, so a window computed there reads a stale value).
// The step-5 gate procedure therefore flies the camera; it does not warp.
// ===========================================================================

// One nametable row in pixels. Taken from parallax rather than re-spelled: the
// scroll this proc reads is stated in the same units, and BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX
// already carries the ensure tying it to the plane's own cell height.
pub const BG_STREAM_ROW_PX   = BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX
// Map rows the screen can touch at once: 224/8 = 28 whole rows, plus one because
// a window origin is not row-aligned, so 29 rows are partially visible at worst.
pub const BG_SCREEN_ROWS     = SCREEN_HEIGHT / BG_STREAM_ROW_PX + 1
// Rows the plane holds that the screen cannot show — the total slack available.
pub const BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS = PLANE_V_CELLS - BG_SCREEN_ROWS
// Half of it above the screen, the rest below. CENTRED, not maximal on either
// side: see reason 2 and reason 3 in the header.
pub const BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS  = BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS / 2
// The per-frame row budget, DERIVED from step 4's rate clamp and not tuned.
pub const BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS   = BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP / BG_STREAM_ROW_PX

ensure(BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS > 0,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: the Plane B ring holds {PLANE_V_CELLS} rows and the screen can touch {BG_SCREEN_ROWS} of them at once, so there is no slack at all (BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS = {BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS}). BG_Stream_Update's containment proof needs at least one spare row; at zero or below the plane cannot hold the visible window plus any lead and every streamed row would be one the screen is already showing")
ensure(BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS >= 0 && BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS <= BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS is {BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS}, outside [0, {BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS}]. That range IS the containment proof in BG_Stream_Update's header: below 0 the window starts under the screen top and the topmost visible rows hold the wrong map row; above the spare the window ends above the screen bottom and the bottom rows do. Neither shows as a build error and both look like a torn background")
ensure(BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS * BG_STREAM_ROW_PX >= BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_Stream_Update can move the window {BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS} row(s) = {BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS * BG_STREAM_ROW_PX} px a frame, but engine/level/parallax.emp's rate clamp lets the BG scroll move {BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP} px a frame. The tracker would fall further behind on every frame of a sustained vertical run and NEVER catch up — the window lags, the lead is eaten, and the background tears at the trailing edge with nothing in the build to say so. Either raise BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP_ROWS' partner here or lower the rate clamp")
ensure(BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS >= 1,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS is {BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS} — the streamer is allowed no rows at all, so BG_Plane_Top can never move and a map taller than the plane would show its first {PLANE_V_CELLS} rows forever while the scroll ran past them")

// -----------------------------------------------
// BG_Stream_Update — move the Plane B window to follow this frame's BG scroll.
//
// In:  nothing. Reads Region_Current (the crossing's cache), that row's
//      rg_bg_span, Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG (map space, already clamped by
//      Parallax_Step5_Vscroll THIS frame) and BG_Plane_Top.
// Out: nothing. Appends up to BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS row entries to Plane_Buffer and
//      advances BG_Plane_Top by one per ADMITTED entry.
//
// CALL IT AFTER Parallax_Update AND BEFORE VBlank. Both halves matter: the scroll
// it reads is the one Step 5 just produced, and the row entries it appends drain
// in the SAME VBlank as the VSRAM word (VInt_DrawLevel and Vscroll_Write are both
// in the VBlank handler), so the new rows and the scroll that reveals them reach
// the hardware together. A tracker run before Parallax_Update would be one frame
// stale in a way the lead hides — which is worse than a tear, because it would
// only appear when the lead was already spent.
//
// EVERY SHIPPED RELEASE ROW LEAVES EARLY, at the second test: all of OJZ act 1's
// ten release rows take rg_bg_span = 0. The proc costs them 16 bytes of fetch and
// an rts, and moves no window, which is what "nothing visible changes before step
// 6" means on the release shape.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BG_Stream_Update () clobbers(d0-d7/a0-a2) {
        // -- the region, READ and not re-resolved: Parallax_CheckBoundary wrote
        //    this cell earlier in the SAME frame, against the same camera. Null
        //    until the first crossing resolves, and null means the act default,
        //    which means the map is the plane, which means nothing to do. --
        movea.l Region_Current, a0
        cmpa.w  #0, a0
        beq     .done

        // -- max_top = map rows - PLANE_V_CELLS. A 0 span is the act default (the
        //    plane's own height) and a map no taller than the plane has a
        //    one-position window, so both leave with the window where it is. --
        move.w  Region.rg_bg_span(a0), d0           // (Region.rg_bg_span)(a0)
        beq     .done                               // 0 = the act default
        lsr.w   #3, d0                              // span px -> map rows (BG_STREAM_ROW_PX)
        subi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS, d0
        ble     .done                               // map is not taller than the plane
        move.w  d0, d3                              // d3 = max_top

        // -- want_top = clamp(vrow - LEAD, 0, max_top). The scroll is non-negative
        //    on every path (step 4's position clamp floors it at 0), so lsr is the
        //    right shift and the only sign question is the subtraction below. --
        move.w  Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG, d6     // (Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG).w
        lsr.w   #3, d6                              // -> the top visible MAP row
        subi.w  #BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS, d6
        bpl     .want_hi
        moveq   #0, d6                              // near the map top: no room for the lead
    .want_hi:
        cmp.w   d3, d6
        ble     .want_ok
        move.w  d3, d6                              // near the map bottom: ditto
    .want_ok:
        // -- direction and budget. THE ORDER HERE IS LOAD-BEARING: moveq writes the
        //    condition codes, so both moveq must precede the cmp whose flags the
        //    beq and the blt read. Written the other way round the blt tests the
        //    moveq's result and the streamer walks the window the wrong way. --
        move.w  BG_Plane_Top, d4                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w — where it is
        moveq   #BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS, d5             // rows left to spend this frame
        moveq   #1, d7                              // step: downward (top increasing)
        cmp.w   d6, d4
        beq     .done                               // already there — the common case
        blt     .loop
        moveq   #-1, d7                             // upward (top decreasing)
    .loop:
        // -- the map row ENTERING the window. Going down the window [T, T+63]
        //    becomes [T+1, T+64], so the new row is T + PLANE_V_CELLS; going up it
        //    becomes [T-1, T+62] and the new row is T - 1. --
        move.w  d4, d1
        tst.w   d7
        bmi     .ent_up
        addi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS, d1
        jbra    .ent_ok
    .ent_up:
        subq.w  #1, d1
    .ent_ok:
        move.w  d1, d0
        andi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS-1, d0                // plane row = map row mod the ring
        moveq   #0, d2                              // the band's layout X: part 2's bands
                                                    //   are all WRAP over a map exactly as
                                                    //   wide as the plane, where the anchor
                                                    //   is structurally 0 (Draw_BG_TileRow)
        jbsr    Draw_BG_TileRow                     // d0 = 1 admitted, 0 refused
        tst.w   d0
        beq     .done                               // REFUSED: do not advance the window.
                                                    //   The cursor advances on 1 and only on
                                                    //   1 — that contract is the whole reason
                                                    //   this producer returns anything.
        add.w   d7, d4
        move.w  d4, BG_Plane_Top                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w
        cmp.w   d6, d4
        beq     .done
        subq.w  #1, d5
        bne     .loop
    .done:
        rts
}

// §4.2: BG_RedrawForSection deleted. Plane B is redrawn atomically alongside
// Plane A via Section_RedrawPlanes (see engine/level/section.emp), triggered
// by the Section_Plane_Dirty flag at level init and cache recovery only.
// Teleports no longer set it: they are pure coordinate rebases — world
// coordinates, plane mapping (mod 64) and scroll (mod 512) are all invariant
// under the $1000px shift, so a redraw would write byte-identical content
// (docs/research/teleport-rebase.md).

// ---- THE PLANE GEOMETRY FAMILY, pinned across ALL its members in this file (2026-09-11) ----
// The LS-8a plane-wrap pins at the end of engine/level/section.emp and plane_buffer.emp named
// this file as hand-spelling the same PLANE_H_CELLS x PLANE_V_CELLS geometry, unpinned. The
// sites were enumerated by what TOUCHES the plane size (every constant and instruction whose
// value must move if the plane does, read over the whole file), not by grepping a literal.
//
// THE TWO PER-AXIS PINS WERE DELETED ON 2026-09-15 (regions part 2, step 2) BECAUSE THEIR
// SITES WERE. They named three instructions, all of them in BG_Init's nametable blit and all
// of them artefacts of the column-major order: `move.w #$8F80` and `cmpi.w #64` (horizontal)
// and `moveq #32-1` (vertical). The row-major blit is one linear `move.l` run, so its three
// remaining literals are the autoincrement $02 (a CELL, not a plane), the plane's VRAM base
// (a layout constant, not a size) and `BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1`, which FOLLOWS the constant and
// so needs no pin at all. Deleting them is not a loosening: an `ensure` whose enumerated
// sites no longer exist cannot fail on the change it names, and leaving it would have read as
// coverage this file no longer needs (docs/DEFERRED_WORK's vacuous-gate rule).
//
// BG_LAYOUT_SIZE keeps its OWN pin, and it is the only one left here: it depends on both axes
// and is still hand-spelled. It was already separate for this reason — folded into the axis
// pins, a vertical-only move fired the HORIZONTAL message too and blamed an axis that had not
// moved (measured 2026-09-11, M25/M26).
ensure(BG_LAYOUT_SIZE == PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2,
       "BG LAYOUT SIZE (engine/level/bg.emp, 2026-09-11). engine/level/bg.emp's BG_LAYOUT_SIZE no longer matches the plane: it is {BG_LAYOUT_SIZE} bytes, and a full Plane B nametable is PLANE_H_CELLS x PLANE_V_CELLS x 2 = {PLANE_H_CELLS} x {PLANE_V_CELLS} x 2 = {PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2}. THE SITE: `pub const BG_LAYOUT_SIZE = 64*64*2`, which BG_Init's nametable blit drains whole as one linear run, which Section_RedrawPlanes' plane-B blit drains the same way, which Draw_BG_TileRow (engine/level/plane_buffer.emp) reads one ROW of, and which games/sonic4/data/levels/ojz/act1/act_assets.emp uses as the embed type of OJZ_Act1_BG_Layout. IT IS NOW THE ONLY PIN IN THIS FILE: the two per-axis pins that stood beside it were deleted in regions part 2 step 2 with the three column-major literals they enumerated (see the family header above), so nothing fires beside this one any more and a plane-size move reaches this file through this message alone. WHAT THIS PIN DOES NOT COVER: the layout's ROW-MAJOR order, which no size can express — and which is exactly what a wrong-order blob would get wrong while passing this check; the count BG_Init and Section_RedrawPlanes spell from it (BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1 and PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS / 2 - 1), which follow their constants; and the out-of-assembler writers of the blob (tools/inject_editor_bg.py, tools/ojz_strip_gen.py), which this pin cannot see")

// ---- THE BG PLANE TRACKER's one-word clear, pinned (regions part 2 step 3) ----
// Both synchronous writers of Plane B — BG_Init above and Section_RedrawPlanes
// (engine/level/section.emp) — retire the wipe state with a single `clr.w
// BG_Wipe_Cursor`, which is only "cursor AND direction" while the two bytes are
// adjacent in that order. A field inserted between them would not fail to build; it
// would leave BG_Wipe_Dir holding whatever the previous act left there while the
// cursor read 0, which is invisible until step 6 arms a sweep and then picks a
// direction from a stale byte. Pinned rather than spelled as two `clr.b` because the
// one-word form is what the two sites actually emit.
ensure(extern("BG_Wipe_Dir") - extern("BG_Wipe_Cursor") == 1,
       "engine/ram.emp's BG_Wipe_Cursor and BG_Wipe_Dir are no longer adjacent bytes in that order (BG_Wipe_Dir - BG_Wipe_Cursor is not 1). BOTH synchronous Plane B writers clear them with ONE `clr.w BG_Wipe_Cursor` — engine/level/bg.emp's BG_Init and engine/level/section.emp's Section_RedrawPlanes — so the pair must stay a word. Either restore the adjacency or respell both sites as two `clr.b`")
